WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:08.120
- Hello everyone, welcome to
Khan Academy's Daily Homeroom.

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:11.820
For those of y'all who aren't
familiar with what this is,

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.810
ever since we had the mass school closures

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:16.800
because of the COVID-19

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.850
all of us at Khan Academy
as a not-for-profit

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:21.210
with a mission of providing
a free world-class education

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:23.690
for anyone anywhere
realized that it's our duty

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:26.510
to do whatever we can to support students,

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:28.810
parents, and teachers through this crisis.

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:31.790
So, obviously over many years
we've been building resources

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:33.730
in math, and English and language arts,

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:35.360
and early learning, Khan Academy Kids,

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.680
you can see Kodi Bear right behind me,

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:38.513
going all the way through

00:00:38.513 --> 00:00:41.670
high school level sciences and SAT prep,

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:43.720
but we realized once the schools

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:46.100
were going to get closed
that people needed even more.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.180
So we've been doing webinars
for parents and teachers

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:50.910
to understand how to structure things.

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:53.500
We've been releasing
schedules on what students,

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:54.720
how they could structure their days

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:56.480
based on different age groups,

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:58.260
and we've been doing this live stream,

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:00.730
this Daily Homeroom so
we can feel connected

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:03.210
in this time of social distancing.

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:06.240
We do have a cool announcement.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.880
Just yesterday we released
what we're calling

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.567
Learning Plans on Khan Academy

00:01:10.567 --> 00:01:12.700
and I think you're gonna
see some of the links.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:13.810
There you go, the link is right there.

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:14.860
It's magical.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:15.920
There, I can get my,

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:17.820
there you go.
(chuckling)

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:21.100
Felipe is doing a better job,
is getting fancier every day.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:22.930
But if you go there
what you're going to see

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:24.300
is we got a lot of feedback from,

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.347
especially students and parents saying,

00:01:26.347 --> 00:01:28.357
"All right the schedules were useful.

00:01:28.357 --> 00:01:29.767
"I can understand that I can do, maybe,

00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:31.417
"40 minutes of math then take a break,

00:01:31.417 --> 00:01:34.087
"then do 40 minutes of
reading, et cetera, et cetera,

00:01:34.087 --> 00:01:36.117
"but over the course
of the next two months

00:01:36.117 --> 00:01:39.037
"or even five months
as we go through summer

00:01:39.037 --> 00:01:41.627
"what are reasonable goals
for me or for my child?

00:01:41.627 --> 00:01:44.557
"How do I make sure either myself

00:01:44.557 --> 00:01:46.607
"or my child is keeping up

00:01:46.607 --> 00:01:48.510
"so that they're ready
for next school year?"

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:51.380
So this is a week by week learning plan

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:53.740
that sets goals for you,
if you're the student,

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:57.190
or your child if you're
the parent, to be on track.

00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:59.090
And this first Learning Plan we've done

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:00.300
is focused on math.

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:02.410
Khan Academy has much more than just math,

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:04.950
but math is one of
those core subject areas

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:06.490
where it's really valuable to make sure

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:07.620
that you don't fall behind.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:09.800
Obviously, even before this COVID crisis

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:11.200
we have talked a lot about

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.070
the importance of mastery learning,

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:14.980
about not being pushed ahead

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:17.310
while you still have gaps
that accumulate over time,

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:18.430
these Swiss cheese gaps,

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:20.500
and Khan Academy can help fill you in.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:22.410
And then you could
imagine with five months

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:26.280
of not being in school now
it's that much more important

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.160
to fill in those gaps
and to keep learning.

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:31.950
And just to underline that
our partners at the NWEA,

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:33.630
they're a non-profit assessment body,

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:36.590
they just released a report yesterday

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:38.010
'cause they have historical data

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:40.310
on what's known as the
summer learning loss,

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:41.570
or the summer slide.

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:43.840
Normally you have three months
of loss over the summer,

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:46.120
not just not learning,
but oftentimes forgetting,

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:47.310
now it might be five months.

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:49.080
And in that world it could be as much

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:50.660
as a year of learning loss.

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:52.950
So please take a look
at those learning plans

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:54.530
and give us feedback.

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:55.830
The other thing I'd like to,

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:58.280
just out of a shout out, a thank you,

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:00.220
Khan Academy, we are not-for-profit,

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:02.560
we are funded by philanthropic donations,

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:04.630
you own as much of Khan Academy as I,

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:06.350
or anyone else, it has no ownership,

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:09.130
it's a public charity,
and we need your support.

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:11.540
Even before the crisis we
were running at a deficit

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:14.060
and now our server costs, et cetera,

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:15.920
are going up many fold,

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:17.520
and we're trying to put out more support.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.600
So if you're in a position to do so

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:21.060
please think about donating.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:22.510
I do wanna give a special thanks

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:25.040
to several corporations
who've stepped up recently

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:29.990
including Bank of America,
AT&amp;T, Google.org, and Novartis.

00:03:29.990 --> 00:03:32.080
That's been a major
help, but we need more.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:35.620
We're still running at a
deficit to do all of this.

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:38.370
So with that, today's live stream

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:39.810
I thought that we could
have a little bit of fun.

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:41.460
We have fun all the time,

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:43.780
but we're gonna keep it
a little bit freeform.

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:45.280
If you're watching this over YouTube,

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:46.610
or you're watching it over Facebook,

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:50.270
put any question you might
possibly be curious about there.

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:52.720
We have team members
that are looking at 'em,

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:54.340
and everything is fair game.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:57.130
Dan is here and he's gonna help me out,

00:03:57.130 --> 00:03:59.040
identifying what folks are saying.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:01.190
But, you know, we're more than happy

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:03.650
to answer questions about some
of what I just talked about.

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:05.410
How do you structure your day?

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:07.480
Questions about learning and education.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:10.880
We're happy to at least
think about questions about

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:13.560
some of the healthcare
stuff that's going on

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:16.310
as much as we are qualified to do so.

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:17.910
Economic things, or just other things

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:19.580
that are going on in your life.

00:04:19.580 --> 00:04:20.550
We're gonna, we're gonna...

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:21.610
Inside our organization

00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:23.470
we have these things
called "Ask Me Anything".

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:26.020
We're gonna make this an
Ask Me Anything version

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:30.780
of our life stream, so
ask us literally anything.

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:31.613
And let's see.

00:04:31.613 --> 00:04:33.600
I'm gonna see some of our team members

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:35.480
are starting to put out.

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:37.920
So Susanna from YouTube,

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:39.850
Susanna Garcia Dominguez,

00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:41.366
and Susanna you've been
asking good questions

00:04:41.366 --> 00:04:43.220
on this live stream (chuckles)

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:46.237
I remember your name off
of YouTube, is asking

00:04:46.237 --> 00:04:49.370
"Will you be releasing a
learning plan for first grade?"

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:51.750
So that is an excellent question.

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:53.200
So your question by itself

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:55.220
is a little bit of a push for us to do it.

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:58.640
First grade is an
interesting overlap subject

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:01.310
or grade level for us
because Khan Academy Kids

00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:02.910
goes up through first grade.

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:06.240
And then the Khan Academy
that is not Khan Academy Kids,

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.310
we have to come up with
a better name for that

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:11.070
'cause that's still for kids and adults,

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:13.870
it starts at that kindergarten
first grade level.

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:15.970
So I think what your question
is a really good push.

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:17.610
Hopefully over the next
couple of days or weeks

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:19.980
we can work with the
Khan Academy Kids team

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:21.430
to maybe put something together

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:23.010
or put something together that's a hybrid

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:25.900
of Khan Academy Kids and the math,

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:28.062
'cause these first learning
plans are math focused

00:05:28.062 --> 00:05:31.960
on non-Khan Academy Kids
Khan Academy. (chuckling)

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:32.793
All right.

00:05:32.793 --> 00:05:37.127
From Facebook Jacqui Riquelme says,

00:05:37.127 --> 00:05:38.787
"Could you please share
what software you use

00:05:38.787 --> 00:05:39.887
"to record video lessons?

00:05:39.887 --> 00:05:41.367
"I would love to use the writing tool

00:05:41.367 --> 00:05:44.047
"to explain in better details
with video for my students.

00:05:44.047 --> 00:05:45.890
"Thank you so much."

00:05:45.890 --> 00:05:48.440
So Jacqui it sounds like
you might be a teacher,

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:50.630
and I know a lot of teachers
even before this crisis

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:52.490
were interested in making,
I guess you could say,

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:53.810
Khan Academy style videos.

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:55.420
And especially with school closures

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:56.380
it's even more important.

00:05:56.380 --> 00:06:00.510
So, what I use, and it's
good that it's right here.

00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:02.450
So, let me see if I can

00:06:02.450 --> 00:06:04.090
show this to you,

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:04.923
I'm picking it up.
(mic thudding)

00:06:04.923 --> 00:06:06.240
There's a little bit, ugh,

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:08.330
I'm bumping into all sorts of stuff.

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:11.570
This is a Wacom tablet I use.

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:13.320
I've upgraded over the years.

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:16.170
I use to have a, kind
of a smaller $80 one.

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:18.820
I think this one costs about $200.

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:23.180
And it will come with a
pen that looks like this.

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:27.640
And so that's what I use
to write when I'm doing it.

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:29.090
This microphone that you see here,

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:30.570
this is the microphone that I use.

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:33.290
And, once again, all of
this is kind of upgrade.

00:06:33.290 --> 00:06:37.220
You don't needs these types of
things to do a decent video.

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:39.360
If you just have even, you know,

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:41.130
your regular headphone microphone

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:42.050
that's probably sufficient.

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:43.970
In the first several years of Khan Academy

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:48.000
I had a $80 tablet and
I just used a standard,

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:50.380
you know, $20 headset that you could find.

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:53.290
And I use an art program.

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:56.410
In the early days I was using
Microsoft Paint (chuckling)

00:06:56.410 --> 00:06:59.630
now I use SketchBook, SketchBook Pro.,

00:06:59.630 --> 00:07:01.400
which is just a nice drawing application.

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:03.860
And the way that I capture the drawing

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:08.170
and my voice is I use
Camtasia to screen capture.

00:07:08.170 --> 00:07:09.650
And Camtasia isn't the only tool,

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:11.590
there's other tools that can allow you

00:07:11.590 --> 00:07:13.240
to do some screen capture.

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:14.073
And I just,

00:07:14.073 --> 00:07:16.000
what I do is I capture a portion.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:20.840
I do a 1280x720 pixel
rectangle of my art tool,

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:23.460
and that's why you don't see
all the widgets and the tools

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:25.950
'cause they're outside
of the recording window.

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:29.033
So that's what I do, I just click record.

00:07:29.033 --> 00:07:31.160
I try to do things fairly informally.

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:33.000
I think it's really valuable
when you're making videos

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:35.050
that it's not overly scripted

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:37.820
because I think the listener can detect

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:40.310
if you are going through
it with them or not,

00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:42.660
so I try to keep it
reasonably extemporaneous

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:45.240
and low-key, but whatever works for you.

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:48.923
But, I encourage you to
definitely keep going with that.

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:51.600
All right.

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:54.687
So from Facebook Rick Cornett asks,

00:07:54.687 --> 00:07:56.407
"How can I set up a science curriculum

00:07:56.407 --> 00:07:58.047
"for my sixth and seventh grader?

00:07:58.047 --> 00:08:01.020
"Math is set up by grade
level, but science is not."

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:02.710
That's a great question, Rick.

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:05.560
So Khan Academy, we don't yet.

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:07.300
It is our aspirations.

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:10.120
We don't yet have an
explicit science curriculum

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:11.900
for middle school students.

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:13.890
With that said, and I've said this

00:08:13.890 --> 00:08:15.690
a couple of times on the live stream,

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:19.130
I think a sixth grader, or
a seventh grader is ready

00:08:19.130 --> 00:08:23.140
to start engaging on parts
of the high school sciences.

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:25.240
So for example, high school biology,

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:26.730
which is on Khan Academy.

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:28.720
I am confident that your children,

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:30.470
especially if you're
there to support them,

00:08:30.470 --> 00:08:34.560
can start learning the basics of biology.

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:36.560
And frankly, it's very relevant right now

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:40.380
to learn about DNA,
RNA, viruses, bacteria,

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:42.240
evolution, natural selection.

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:43.840
Obviously, with the viruses,

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:45.730
evolution and natural
selection is happening

00:08:45.730 --> 00:08:47.870
before our eyes in real time.

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:50.210
So I would definitely look
at the high school biology

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:51.990
as a great thing for potentially sixth

00:08:51.990 --> 00:08:52.823
or seventh graders.

00:08:52.823 --> 00:08:54.520
They definitely have the
math background for it.

00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:56.620
And then even the early units in chemistry

00:08:56.620 --> 00:08:59.560
where you're learning about
the periodic table of elements,

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:01.650
what elements are, the basics of bonding,

00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:03.650
the basics of balancing
chemical equations.

00:09:03.650 --> 00:09:05.960
There's nothing there
that's cognitively beyond

00:09:05.960 --> 00:09:07.310
a sixth or a seventh grader.

00:09:07.310 --> 00:09:08.750
Especially, if they have some supports.

00:09:08.750 --> 00:09:11.480
And physics is a little
bit more mathematical,

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:14.290
but there is even elements
of the physics where,

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:18.550
you know, maybe the one
dimensional motion or Newton's Laws

00:09:18.550 --> 00:09:20.640
could be a really
interesting thing to tackle.

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:22.940
And if your students or your children

00:09:22.940 --> 00:09:23.773
are able to do that now

00:09:23.773 --> 00:09:25.390
they're going to have a serious leg up

00:09:25.390 --> 00:09:29.453
when they get to true
high school level science.

00:09:30.490 --> 00:09:31.323
So let's see.

