00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:40.280
And probably more
importantly people don't know

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:43.053
about this part, which is
who did you go to for help?

00:10:44.740 --> 00:10:46.820
- Who did I go to help when
I started Khan Academy?

00:10:46.820 --> 00:10:49.073
Or, like when I needed
help when I was a kid?

00:10:50.370 --> 00:10:53.823
- Who did you go to for help
when you started Khan Academy?

00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:57.050
- Oh, yeah so for those
of y'all who don't know,

00:10:57.050 --> 00:10:59.470
and you can probably do a YouTube search

00:10:59.470 --> 00:11:02.364
and find videos of me giving
a long winded explanation

00:11:02.364 --> 00:11:05.084
of how Khan Academy started.
(chuckling)

00:11:05.084 --> 00:11:08.000
I've been running off of
that story for a long time.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:10.960
But there's a lot of folks who still don't

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:12.470
maybe know that story as much.

00:11:12.470 --> 00:11:16.510
So back in 2004 I was, I
had just gotten married,

00:11:16.510 --> 00:11:21.060
my original background was
in technology and in math,

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:22.490
but I had gone to business school

00:11:22.490 --> 00:11:25.010
and I was now working
as an investment analyst

00:11:25.010 --> 00:11:26.010
of all things.

00:11:26.010 --> 00:11:29.580
And it just came out of
conversation after my wedding

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:32.430
my 12 year old cousin Nadia,
at the time, was visiting

00:11:32.430 --> 00:11:33.800
and she was having trouble in math.

00:11:33.800 --> 00:11:34.727
And so I said,

00:11:34.727 --> 00:11:38.257
"Nadia I am confident that
you can master mathematics.

00:11:38.257 --> 00:11:40.200
"How about when you go
back to New Orleans",

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:41.787
which is also where I was born,

00:11:41.787 --> 00:11:43.660
"I'm willing to tutor you remotely."

00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:45.100
I was living in Boston at the time.

00:11:45.100 --> 00:11:46.442
And she agreed.

00:11:46.442 --> 00:11:50.330
And she's probably like a lot
of you students listening.

00:11:50.330 --> 00:11:53.420
She was 12 years old, she wanted to learn,

00:11:53.420 --> 00:11:56.350
but she for many reasons
she had accumulated gaps

00:11:56.350 --> 00:11:58.110
in her knowledge and had convinced herself

00:11:58.110 --> 00:12:01.330
that maybe she wasn't
quote: "Good at math."

00:12:01.330 --> 00:12:04.030
And many of you parents might relate,

00:12:04.030 --> 00:12:05.767
her mom, Najudati, she was saying,

00:12:05.767 --> 00:12:07.077
"Yeah I'm worried about Nadia.

00:12:07.077 --> 00:12:09.200
"She's losing her confidence here."

00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:11.010
And so those tutoring sessions,

00:12:11.010 --> 00:12:11.920
I was doing it remotely.

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:13.550
I think a lot of you teachers
are finding yourselves

00:12:13.550 --> 00:12:15.170
in that kind of a circumstance now

00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:16.910
because of the closures.

00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:18.900
And I was getting on the phone.

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:20.760
We didn't have things
like Zoom, and Hangouts,

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:22.400
and live streams back then.

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:24.440
But I was trying to find
ways that we could see

00:12:24.440 --> 00:12:26.760
each other's writing,
and things like that.

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:28.210
But slowly but surely Nadia

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:29.520
was able to catch up with her class.

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:30.610
It was actually unit conversion

00:12:30.610 --> 00:12:32.150
that she was having trouble with.

00:12:32.150 --> 00:12:34.040
Then she actually got a
little bit ahead of her class.

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:36.330
Then I became what I call a "Tiger Cousin"

00:12:36.330 --> 00:12:38.617
and I called up her school, and I said,

00:12:38.617 --> 00:12:40.217
"You know I really think Nadia Rehman

00:12:40.217 --> 00:12:42.027
"should retake that
placement test from last year

00:12:42.027 --> 00:12:43.960
"that put her in that current math track."

00:12:43.960 --> 00:12:45.900
And, you know, they famously
said, "Who are you?"

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:47.280
And I said, "I'm her cousin."

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:50.170
And they let her, (chuckles)
surprisingly take that test.

00:12:50.170 --> 00:12:53.330
And that same Nadia who
thought she wasn't good at math

00:12:53.330 --> 00:12:54.690
only a few months before

00:12:54.690 --> 00:12:56.160
was then put into an advanced class.

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:58.460
And that same Nadia who
thought she couldn't understand

00:12:58.460 --> 00:13:00.170
unit conversion when she was 12

00:13:00.170 --> 00:13:03.560
by the time she was 13 she was
taking college level classes

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:04.840
at the University of New Orleans.

00:13:04.840 --> 00:13:06.080
And I tell you that story

00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:08.720
only partially to show off
on behalf of my cousin,

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:10.900
but more to tell many of you parents

00:13:10.900 --> 00:13:12.130
and many of you students out there

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:16.270
that are thinking that you
are not good at something

00:13:16.270 --> 00:13:18.650
it has nothing to do
with your innate ability.

00:13:18.650 --> 00:13:20.870
The subject matter is not
fundamentally difficult.

00:13:20.870 --> 00:13:22.900
It's likely because you have gaps.

00:13:22.900 --> 00:13:25.940
And that's where Khan Academy
hopefully has your back.

00:13:25.940 --> 00:13:29.440
And the silver lining of what
we're going through right now

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:31.750
over the next two months, and
probably the next five months

00:13:31.750 --> 00:13:34.980
is this is a great opportunity
to leverage Khan Academy

00:13:34.980 --> 00:13:36.140
to fill in those gaps.

00:13:36.140 --> 00:13:38.900
We had Tim Vanderberg
on a couple of days ago,

00:13:38.900 --> 00:13:41.250
amazing teacher out of
Hesperia in California

00:13:41.250 --> 00:13:43.570
who makes all of his sixth
graders start Khan Academy

00:13:43.570 --> 00:13:46.520
at the very basics starting
with the arithmetic course,

00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:48.070
which starts literally at one plus one,

00:13:48.070 --> 00:13:50.330
but if you know that material
you go through it really fast.

00:13:50.330 --> 00:13:51.650
But that's the way that he ensures

00:13:51.650 --> 00:13:52.910
that his kids don't have gaps.

00:13:52.910 --> 00:13:55.840
And he has the student
population that starts the year

00:13:55.840 --> 00:13:58.570
at 90% is well below grade level

00:13:58.570 --> 00:14:00.230
and then he finishes the
year where they're able

00:14:00.230 --> 00:14:02.590
to fill in their gaps and
most of them are accelerated

00:14:02.590 --> 00:14:04.100
or are able to move ahead.

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:06.760
So, anyway going back to the story.

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:07.770
I was kinda hooked.

00:14:07.770 --> 00:14:10.910
I started tutoring Nadia's
younger brothers as well.

00:14:10.910 --> 00:14:12.240
Then word gets around in my family

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:13.930
that free tutoring is going on

00:14:13.930 --> 00:14:17.160
and I found myself (chuckling)
tutoring 10, 15 cousins,

00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:18.770
family friends around the country.

00:14:18.770 --> 00:14:21.460
And Khan Academy, you
know, I had this background

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:24.017
in software engineering and I said,

00:14:24.017 --> 00:14:27.427
"Well there aren't good
resources for my cousins

00:14:27.427 --> 00:14:29.767
"to get practice and immediate feedback.

00:14:29.767 --> 00:14:31.507
"What if I could make some of that

00:14:31.507 --> 00:14:33.947
"and also provide a dashboard
for me as their coach

00:14:33.947 --> 00:14:35.477
"or their teacher, or their tutor?

00:14:35.477 --> 00:14:37.537
"So that I could understand
where their gaps were.

00:14:37.537 --> 00:14:39.127
"So that I could dig a little bit deeper

00:14:39.127 --> 00:14:41.430
"and assign appropriate
work for them", et cetera.

00:14:41.430 --> 00:14:43.080
And that was the first Khan Academy.

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:44.510
It had nothing to do with videos,

00:14:44.510 --> 00:14:45.870
which many people associate it with.

00:14:45.870 --> 00:14:49.570
And it was in 2006 I was
showing off this software

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:51.100
to a friend at a dinner party.

00:14:51.100 --> 00:14:53.637
As you can imagine I'm a
very fun dinner party guest

00:14:53.637 --> 00:14:55.640
and I,
(laughing)

00:14:55.640 --> 00:14:56.967
and he said, "Sal this is all cool,

00:14:56.967 --> 00:14:59.350
"but how are you scaling
your actual lessons up?"

00:14:59.350 --> 00:15:01.660
And said, I told him,
his name's Zuli Ramzan,

00:15:01.660 --> 00:15:02.880
I have to give him full credit,

00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:05.417
I said, "Zuli, it's hard
to scale my lessons.

00:15:05.417 --> 00:15:07.377
"I feel like I'm repeating the same thing.

00:15:07.377 --> 00:15:08.507
"I'm answering the same question

00:15:08.507 --> 00:15:10.090
"oftentimes over and over."

00:15:10.090 --> 00:15:11.177
And he said, "Well why don't you record

00:15:11.177 --> 00:15:12.877
"some of your lessons as YouTube videos

00:15:12.877 --> 00:15:15.090
"and upload them for your cousins?"

00:15:15.090 --> 00:15:17.660
And I initially thought
it was a horrible idea.

00:15:17.660 --> 00:15:20.310
I said, "No, YouTube is
for cats playing piano."

00:15:20.310 --> 00:15:22.090
But I went home that weekend,

00:15:22.090 --> 00:15:23.890
got over the idea that it wasn't my idea,

00:15:23.890 --> 00:15:24.730
and I gave it a shot.

00:15:24.730 --> 00:15:26.260
And then that took on a life of its own.

00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:28.670
And, you know, I could go more in depth,

00:15:28.670 --> 00:15:31.798
but by 2009 I just had, you
know this was three, four years

00:15:31.798 --> 00:15:34.500
into this cousin project,
five years into it,

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:36.600
I had trouble focusing on my day job.

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:37.870
There were about 100,000 people

00:15:37.870 --> 00:15:38.960
who were using it every month

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:42.687
and I said, "Surely, if I set
this up as a not-for-profit

00:15:42.687 --> 00:15:44.787
"philanthropists will donate to this

00:15:44.787 --> 00:15:48.957
"because the impact we could
have on the world is huge.

00:15:48.957 --> 00:15:50.817
"If we translate it into
the language of the world,

00:15:50.817 --> 00:15:52.210
"go across subjects and grades."

00:15:52.210 --> 00:15:56.480
And, you know whenever you
start anything entrepreneurial

00:15:56.480 --> 00:15:58.880
you have to start with
that delusional optimism

00:15:58.880 --> 00:16:01.190
that surely the world will
conspire to make this happen.

00:16:01.190 --> 00:16:02.730
And you usually realize quite quickly

00:16:02.730 --> 00:16:04.260
that it was a little bit delusional.

00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:07.540
But after about eight, nine
months of living off of savings

00:16:07.540 --> 00:16:10.300
and openly it was probably one
of the most stressful times

00:16:10.300 --> 00:16:12.340
in my life, I had given up a good career,

00:16:12.340 --> 00:16:15.180
and my first child had just been born,

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:16.921
but after about 10 months all of a sudden

00:16:16.921 --> 00:16:19.230
some philanthropists started
to come out of the woodwork,

00:16:19.230 --> 00:16:22.770
and by 2010 we were able to
become a real organization.

00:16:22.770 --> 00:16:23.900
So, anyway.

00:16:23.900 --> 00:16:25.050
You got me on my soap box

00:16:25.050 --> 00:16:26.780
telling the origin story of Khan Academy.

00:16:26.780 --> 00:16:28.410
But I'm happy to answer any
other questions you have

00:16:28.410 --> 00:16:29.593
on that front as well.

00:16:30.460 --> 00:16:31.293
- Sal, there's actually
- So, oh,

00:16:31.293 --> 00:16:32.920
- A follow-up to that.
- Oh yeah.

00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:34.330
- So, on YouTube--
- Follow-up question, good.

00:16:34.330 --> 00:16:35.697
- Yeah, on YouTube Sofia says,

00:16:35.697 --> 00:16:37.520
"Great, where is Nadia now?"

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:39.803
and she also has a sister called Nadia.

00:16:40.890 --> 00:16:42.080
- Oh (chuckling) very good.

00:16:42.080 --> 00:16:45.580
So Nadia is now, if I'm
doing the math correctly,

00:16:45.580 --> 00:16:49.320
she is 28 years old,
she lives in New York.

00:16:49.320 --> 00:16:51.900
I was just Zoom conference calling

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:54.330
with her and her family
a couple of nights ago.

00:16:54.330 --> 00:16:56.660
Obviously, everyone's
worried about the situation,

00:16:56.660 --> 00:16:58.080
especially in New York.

00:16:58.080 --> 00:17:01.480
But she is doing a masters,

00:17:01.480 --> 00:17:04.620
and hopefully a PhD as well,
in clinical psychology.

00:17:04.620 --> 00:17:08.630
She wants to become a
clinical psychologist,

00:17:08.630 --> 00:17:11.270
therapist, type person.

00:17:11.270 --> 00:17:13.590
So she is, we're very proud of her.

00:17:13.590 --> 00:17:14.450
She is doing well.

00:17:14.450 --> 00:17:15.750
Although I often joke with her

00:17:15.750 --> 00:17:17.623
that there's a lot riding on her success.

00:17:17.623 --> 00:17:18.456
(laughing)

00:17:18.456 --> 00:17:21.320
That if that early intervention,

00:17:21.320 --> 00:17:23.120
you know, if Nadia ends up,

00:17:23.120 --> 00:17:24.380
anyway,

00:17:24.380 --> 00:17:26.203
yeah, she's on track.

00:17:28.360 --> 00:17:30.400
- And, Sal, Facebook,

00:17:30.400 --> 00:17:32.057
Scott Yang on Facebook asks,

00:17:32.057 --> 00:17:33.843
"Why is there a giant bear behind you?"

00:17:33.843 --> 00:17:36.623
(chuckles) I don't think
everyone knows who Kodi is.

00:17:37.460 --> 00:17:39.670
- There's a giant bear behind me?

00:17:39.670 --> 00:17:40.503
Oh, yeah and you're right.
(Dan chuckling)

00:17:40.503 --> 00:17:41.952
There's a giant bear behind me.

00:17:41.952 --> 00:17:43.552
(laughing)

00:17:43.552 --> 00:17:44.385
No.

00:17:44.385 --> 00:17:47.350
So, my mom has actually kept saying,

00:17:47.350 --> 00:17:49.417
she's like, "You know I
like these live streams,

00:17:49.417 --> 00:17:51.917
"but your background is really horrible.

00:17:51.917 --> 00:17:54.197
"Like, you have some random
junk behind you, Sal.

00:17:54.197 --> 00:17:55.906
"You have to fix your background."

00:17:55.906 --> 00:17:56.739
(chuckling)

00:17:56.739 --> 00:18:00.080
And so, I actually went
looking this morning.

00:18:00.080 --> 00:18:03.480
Right before this I was actually on CNN,

00:18:03.480 --> 00:18:04.313
and my mom's like,

00:18:04.313 --> 00:18:05.146
"If you're gonna be on CNN

00:18:05.146 --> 00:18:06.740
"you gotta get a good background, Sal."

00:18:06.740 --> 00:18:08.870
And I (chuckles) I just
started digging around

00:18:08.870 --> 00:18:11.440
for what we have that could
make a decent background.

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:12.610
And this is what I found.

00:18:12.610 --> 00:18:13.960
And for those of you who don't know,

00:18:13.960 --> 00:18:17.700
this is Kodi Bear gazing over my shoulder.

00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:21.530
He's kind of the primary
character in Khan Academy Kids.

00:18:21.530 --> 00:18:26.530
So, this is a reminder that
that's available for you.

00:18:26.589 --> 00:18:28.160
And,

00:18:28.160 --> 00:18:29.010
and yeah,

00:18:29.010 --> 00:18:31.830
and maybe having Kodi glare at you

00:18:31.830 --> 00:18:33.813
will be a reminder to keep learning.

00:18:38.220 --> 00:18:39.260
So, let's see.

00:18:39.260 --> 00:18:41.230
There's a bunch of questions
I'm seeing here, Dan,

00:18:41.230 --> 00:18:42.370
but feel free to jump in with more.

00:18:42.370 --> 00:18:44.077
I see YouTube, Arev Jane says,

00:18:44.077 --> 00:18:45.837
"What do I do when my brain gets stressed

00:18:45.837 --> 00:18:48.560
"in reading or math?"

00:18:48.560 --> 00:18:51.270
So, there's a lot to unpack

00:18:51.270 --> 00:18:54.220
what that might be.

00:18:54.220 --> 00:18:55.450
One thing that I've talked a lot about

00:18:55.450 --> 00:18:56.730
in this live stream and other places

00:18:56.730 --> 00:18:58.330
is just the value of meditation.

00:18:58.330 --> 00:19:01.170
And meditation, it does not
have to be something fancy.

00:19:01.170 --> 00:19:03.310
It literally can be, you know,

00:19:03.310 --> 00:19:05.580
before you embark on something

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:08.750
give yourself two minutes
and just sit there

00:19:08.750 --> 00:19:11.000
and close your eyes if it's useful,

00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:13.570
soften your gaze, and just try to just,

00:19:13.570 --> 00:19:15.030
try to observe your thoughts.

00:19:15.030 --> 00:19:16.883
And what you find is the more you do it,

00:19:16.883 --> 00:19:19.130
and the more consistently you do it

00:19:19.130 --> 00:19:21.560
you start to realize that
you aren't your thoughts.

00:19:21.560 --> 00:19:23.260
That you can observe your thoughts.

00:19:23.260 --> 00:19:25.560
And the more that you
can observe your thoughts

00:19:25.560 --> 00:19:29.920
the less overwhelming
that they can become.

00:19:29.920 --> 00:19:31.820
And, you know, I think we've all felt

00:19:31.820 --> 00:19:33.400
some of what you might be feeling Arev,

00:19:33.400 --> 00:19:35.360
which is, you know,
you're reading something

00:19:35.360 --> 00:19:36.193
and you're like,

00:19:36.193 --> 00:19:37.026
"Wait, wait did I
understand that properly?"

00:19:37.026 --> 00:19:38.710
and you keep rereading that same sentence.

00:19:38.710 --> 00:19:40.210
I used to do that a lot.

00:19:40.210 --> 00:19:43.010
I think in math, you know,
you're in a test or something,

00:19:43.010 --> 00:19:44.380
or you're doing a problem and you're like,

00:19:44.380 --> 00:19:46.077
you know there's a process
in your brain that's saying,

00:19:46.077 --> 00:19:46.997
"Oh well you know how to do it.

00:19:46.997 --> 00:19:47.830
"This is hard.

00:19:47.830 --> 00:19:48.717
"What if you don't get this answer right?

00:19:48.717 --> 00:19:49.880
"You might fail your math class."

00:19:49.880 --> 00:19:52.820
and then that becomes a
little bit debilitating

00:19:52.820 --> 00:19:54.930
'cause it doesn't allow
your brain to enter

00:19:54.930 --> 00:19:56.410
into a kind of state of flow.

00:19:56.410 --> 00:20:00.070
So that's one tip I have is,
you know, just meditation.

00:20:00.070 --> 00:20:02.380
Actually, physical exercise
can be really good.

00:20:02.380 --> 00:20:04.220
There is some evidence,
actually a lot of evidence,

00:20:04.220 --> 00:20:05.340
that just getting that brain flowing,

00:20:05.340 --> 00:20:08.070
running, et cetera, it
releases those endorphins,

00:20:08.070 --> 00:20:11.340
and then when you sit down
to do something like reading,

00:20:11.340 --> 00:20:14.160
or math, or anything that
you might find stressful

00:20:14.160 --> 00:20:15.467
in your life you just realize that,

00:20:15.467 --> 00:20:18.540
"Oh, you know, I'm just gonna
do it, see what happens."

00:20:18.540 --> 00:20:19.960
So I think the more that
you just give yourself

00:20:19.960 --> 00:20:22.290
permission to be, be in the moment,

00:20:22.290 --> 00:20:23.470
don't try to think too much about

00:20:23.470 --> 00:20:24.420
what's happened in the past

00:20:24.420 --> 00:20:25.520
or what might happen in the future,

00:20:25.520 --> 00:20:27.687
but just like, "Oh this is fun, I'm here.

00:20:27.687 --> 00:20:30.057
"I'm this sentient being in the universe

00:20:30.057 --> 00:20:31.997
"that is going to read
this entertaining book

00:20:31.997 --> 00:20:34.397
"or that gets to puzzle through

00:20:34.397 --> 00:20:36.150
"these interesting math questions."

00:20:36.150 --> 00:20:40.680
I think you have that attitude
and it can really help.

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:43.390
You know, I've considered myself lucky

00:20:43.390 --> 00:20:48.270
that throughout my life I've
always taken a fun attitude,

00:20:48.270 --> 00:20:49.860
kind of an adventurous attitude

00:20:49.860 --> 00:20:52.090
whenever I see a test or something

00:20:52.090 --> 00:20:53.730
and I think it's really helped me.

00:20:53.730 --> 00:20:56.170
It's like, "Oh this is fun,
it's a bunch of puzzles."

00:20:56.170 --> 00:20:57.700
And I don't try to think too much

00:20:57.700 --> 00:20:59.710
about what the outcomes might be

00:20:59.710 --> 00:21:02.360
because that can be a little
bit stressful sometimes.

00:21:04.200 --> 00:21:05.110
- Sal that was wonderful.

00:21:05.110 --> 00:21:08.557
We have a related question
from Selena Chang on YouTube.

00:21:08.557 --> 00:21:11.347
"Hi Sal, how can I find a passion project

00:21:11.347 --> 00:21:12.997
"to pursue while in high school?"

00:21:14.900 --> 00:21:16.490
- Great question, Selena.

00:21:16.490 --> 00:21:19.229
So, meditation might help there.

00:21:19.229 --> 00:21:21.200
You know, you might
(chuckling)

00:21:21.200 --> 00:21:23.646
sit under a tree for a little while

00:21:23.646 --> 00:21:26.220
and have an enlightenment experience.

00:21:26.220 --> 00:21:29.050
Or, what I would do is really reflect,

00:21:29.050 --> 00:21:31.810
like keep a lookout in the world for

00:21:31.810 --> 00:21:33.960
where are there problems to be solved.

00:21:33.960 --> 00:21:37.360
And this crisis we're in is highlighting

00:21:37.360 --> 00:21:39.120
a lot of problems that have always existed

00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:41.640
and they're introducing a set of new ones.

00:21:41.640 --> 00:21:42.890
I've talked at this live stream,

00:21:42.890 --> 00:21:44.630
I think there's many opportunities

00:21:44.630 --> 00:21:46.500
for service projects right now.

00:21:46.500 --> 00:21:49.590
It could be figuring out safe ways

00:21:49.590 --> 00:21:51.630
to get groceries for, say, elderly people

00:21:51.630 --> 00:21:53.130
in your community.

00:21:53.130 --> 00:21:54.540
Ways to get food.

00:21:54.540 --> 00:21:56.490
You know, we're going
through a financial situation

00:21:56.490 --> 00:21:58.450
where a lot of people
have lost their jobs.

00:21:58.450 --> 00:22:00.030
And I never thought it could be this way

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:01.310
in the United States, but there could be,

00:22:01.310 --> 00:22:04.770
there's people probably not
too far from where you live

00:22:04.770 --> 00:22:06.440
who are worried about
where their next meal

00:22:06.440 --> 00:22:07.273
is going to come from.

00:22:07.273 --> 00:22:09.470
So are there ways to coordinate, you know,

00:22:09.470 --> 00:22:11.670
just dropping off the basics to folks,

00:22:11.670 --> 00:22:14.250
milk, eggs, bread, things like that.

00:22:14.250 --> 00:22:16.610
Are there ways to help

00:22:16.610 --> 00:22:20.503
with some of the social
isolation people are feeling?

00:22:21.890 --> 00:22:24.670
My mom, she's living by
herself in New Orleans,

00:22:24.670 --> 00:22:26.910
and you know, she's the age population

00:22:26.910 --> 00:22:28.100
that's high-risk for COVID,

00:22:28.100 --> 00:22:29.680
and she's watching the news all day

00:22:29.680 --> 00:22:30.830
and she's getting stressed,

00:22:30.830 --> 00:22:33.340
so I've been telling, you
know, it's always important

00:22:33.340 --> 00:22:34.760
for me to keep in touch with my mom,

00:22:34.760 --> 00:22:37.230
but even more, like, for her mental health

00:22:37.230 --> 00:22:39.567
it's super important for
me to keep connected,

00:22:39.567 --> 00:22:41.140
and that she can see my kids,

00:22:41.140 --> 00:22:43.320
and that she doesn't feel isolated.

00:22:43.320 --> 00:22:45.670
But there's a lot of
people in her situation

00:22:45.670 --> 00:22:46.900
who might be a little bit older,

00:22:46.900 --> 00:22:49.720
who are alone at home,
who might not have someone

00:22:49.720 --> 00:22:53.277
who is checking in on
them on a daily basis.

00:22:53.277 --> 00:22:56.170
Maybe there's opportunities around that.

00:22:56.170 --> 00:22:59.900
If you're more inclined on
the invention side of things,

00:22:59.900 --> 00:23:02.960
you know, are there gloves you can create

00:23:02.960 --> 00:23:05.670
that not only protect
you, but disinfect things

00:23:05.670 --> 00:23:06.710
as you touch them, you know?

00:23:06.710 --> 00:23:09.700
You can imagine a glove that
is made of some material,

00:23:09.700 --> 00:23:11.120
or is doused in some material

00:23:11.120 --> 00:23:14.260
that when you use it not
only does it protect you,

00:23:14.260 --> 00:23:15.670
but it protects other people as well.

00:23:15.670 --> 00:23:19.670
I mean, you can keep
imagining more and more things

00:23:20.620 --> 00:23:22.581
that could help solve a lot of problems

00:23:22.581 --> 00:23:23.760
for the world right now,

00:23:23.760 --> 00:23:26.110
or it could've solved problems
that existed even before.

00:23:26.110 --> 00:23:27.840
But I would just reflect on that.

00:23:27.840 --> 00:23:29.700
And then when you feel some
energy around something

00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:31.100
just run with it.

00:23:31.100 --> 00:23:33.060
And as I've talked about
in other live streams

00:23:33.060 --> 00:23:33.960
there's an opportunity here.

00:23:33.960 --> 00:23:36.240
The silver lining is a lot of the things

00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:38.670
that historically keep
young people super busy

00:23:38.670 --> 00:23:41.190
with, you know, hours
of homework every night

00:23:41.190 --> 00:23:43.500
and this practice and that practice,

00:23:43.500 --> 00:23:44.720
a lot of that's gone now.

00:23:44.720 --> 00:23:46.050
And so you have more time

00:23:46.050 --> 00:23:47.740
to be able to dig deep into something.

00:23:47.740 --> 00:23:50.120
You know, if it's coding there's
resources on Khan Academy.

00:23:50.120 --> 00:23:51.820
And people talked about
project-based learning,

00:23:51.820 --> 00:23:53.840
that is one area where Khan Academy has

00:23:53.840 --> 00:23:56.010
what I would describe as
project-based learning

00:23:56.010 --> 00:23:57.660
to be able to code and create things.

00:23:57.660 --> 00:24:00.390
You can make apps to solve
interesting problems.

00:24:00.390 --> 00:24:02.930
Ah, so yeah, the world's
your oyster right now.

00:24:02.930 --> 00:24:05.330
It's a great opportunity
to do that type of thing.

00:24:05.330 --> 00:24:06.780
But make sure it's something
that appeals to you.

00:24:06.780 --> 00:24:08.240
You're not trying to
do it for other people,

00:24:08.240 --> 00:24:09.073
you're not saying,

00:24:09.073 --> 00:24:11.570
"Oh how will this look on
a college application?"

00:24:11.570 --> 00:24:13.130
Do it because it's authentically something

00:24:13.130 --> 00:24:14.370
you're interested in, and by the way,

00:24:14.370 --> 00:24:15.990
if you do that it actually will look good

00:24:15.990 --> 00:24:17.450
on a college application.
(chuckling)

00:24:17.450 --> 00:24:19.900
'Cause it'll be truly you
and it'll be authentic.

00:24:22.940 --> 00:24:25.540
So, there are questions.

00:24:25.540 --> 00:24:27.917
Let's see, from Facebook
Sandra Edwards asks:

00:24:27.917 --> 00:24:30.347
"So much has changed over the past month.

00:24:30.347 --> 00:24:32.387
"What positive changes have you noticed

00:24:32.387 --> 00:24:35.887
"coinciding with more
people being online?"

00:24:37.130 --> 00:24:38.260
Well,

00:24:38.260 --> 00:24:39.093
you know,

00:24:39.093 --> 00:24:41.946
it's hard to talk about
too many positive things

00:24:41.946 --> 00:24:44.920
because it's a tough,
tough period right now.

00:24:44.920 --> 00:24:46.630
Some of the positive things I've seen,

00:24:46.630 --> 00:24:49.090
and this transcends Khan Academy

00:24:49.090 --> 00:24:51.220
is, you know, what I just talked about.

00:24:51.220 --> 00:24:53.910
This crisis has made me realize

00:24:53.910 --> 00:24:55.550
that I have to stay connected with more

00:24:55.550 --> 00:24:56.680
of my friends and family.

00:24:56.680 --> 00:24:59.840
Many of whom, I might have
not spoken to for a while.

00:24:59.840 --> 00:25:02.390
So I'm making sure that I'm
calling up cousins and uncles,

00:25:02.390 --> 00:25:04.340
and friends from high school.

00:25:04.340 --> 00:25:07.150
So in a strange way the social distancing

00:25:07.150 --> 00:25:10.960
is making us think beyond just the people

00:25:10.960 --> 00:25:12.770
that we normally see
on a day to day basis.

00:25:12.770 --> 00:25:14.120
So that might be a silver lining,

00:25:14.120 --> 00:25:16.110
that we're connecting
with a lot more people.

00:25:16.110 --> 00:25:19.920
I think this crisis reminds
us of what's really important.

00:25:19.920 --> 00:25:22.510
All of us get caught up in the day to day.

00:25:22.510 --> 00:25:23.417
If you're a student you're like,

00:25:23.417 --> 00:25:24.597
"Oh I got that assignment due.

00:25:24.597 --> 00:25:25.457
"I got that homework.

00:25:25.457 --> 00:25:27.417
"Am I gonna, the SAT, college.

00:25:27.417 --> 00:25:29.080
"What's my career choice?"

00:25:29.080 --> 00:25:30.970
But if you think about it,
those are important things,

00:25:30.970 --> 00:25:33.927
and obviously us adults
we're always thinking about,

00:25:33.927 --> 00:25:36.160
"Oh, you know, that
project I'm working on."

00:25:36.160 --> 00:25:39.600
Or that next goal, or that next event,

00:25:39.600 --> 00:25:40.800
or whatever it might be.

00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:43.020
And all of those things are important,

00:25:43.020 --> 00:25:44.760
but they're not the most important thing.

00:25:44.760 --> 00:25:46.490
The most important thing is your health,

00:25:46.490 --> 00:25:48.450
your mental health, your connections,

00:25:48.450 --> 00:25:49.530
your support networks.

00:25:49.530 --> 00:25:51.040
And I think it's times like this

00:25:51.040 --> 00:25:53.660
when you realize just how fragile we are,

00:25:53.660 --> 00:25:56.960
how fragile in certain
ways society can be,

00:25:56.960 --> 00:26:00.810
that it refocuses you on
the really important things.

00:26:00.810 --> 00:26:02.580
The things that really
fulfill us as human beings

00:26:02.580 --> 00:26:05.100
versus these hoops that
we keep jumping through

00:26:05.100 --> 00:26:07.047
our whole lives and maybe
when we're older we realize,

00:26:07.047 --> 00:26:08.877
"Why did I jump through so many hoops?

00:26:08.877 --> 00:26:11.767
"Why didn't I spend more
time with my parents

00:26:11.767 --> 00:26:12.667
"while they were alive?

00:26:12.667 --> 00:26:14.177
"Why didn't I connect with those cousins

00:26:14.177 --> 00:26:15.400
"when I had a chance?"

00:26:15.400 --> 00:26:16.233
Et cetera, et cetera.

00:26:16.233 --> 00:26:17.066
So I think that is bringing that.

00:26:17.066 --> 00:26:18.920
You know, we're spending
more time with family.

00:26:18.920 --> 00:26:20.020
That's positive.

00:26:20.020 --> 00:26:21.793
On the education side of things,

00:26:22.760 --> 00:26:24.810
you know, this is a very
suboptimal situation,

00:26:24.810 --> 00:26:28.850
but the silver lining is
some of these techniques

00:26:28.850 --> 00:26:31.800
that are having to happen where
students are having to build

00:26:31.800 --> 00:26:32.990
a little bit more independence,

00:26:32.990 --> 00:26:34.830
a little bit more agency,

00:26:34.830 --> 00:26:37.840
and they're still supported
by parents and teachers,

00:26:37.840 --> 00:26:40.610
that skill is actually
a super valuable skill.

00:26:40.610 --> 00:26:43.500
It's more valuable than knowing
how to factor a polynomial,

00:26:43.500 --> 00:26:45.880
is learning how to learn.

00:26:45.880 --> 00:26:47.330
And so I'm hoping for the students

00:26:47.330 --> 00:26:48.490
who are able to build that muscle,

00:26:48.490 --> 00:26:51.130
hopefully many of y'all
are listening right now,

00:26:51.130 --> 00:26:53.230
that's gonna pay dividends
if you can keep applying it

00:26:53.230 --> 00:26:55.960
in the summer, and keep
applying it your whole life.

00:26:55.960 --> 00:26:57.600
Because it's not like, you know,

00:26:57.600 --> 00:27:00.170
the days of going to K through 12,

00:27:00.170 --> 00:27:02.480
and then going to college,
and then just having skills

00:27:02.480 --> 00:27:04.670
that you will use the rest of
your life until you retire.

00:27:04.670 --> 00:27:05.503
Those days are over.

00:27:05.503 --> 00:27:07.140
You're going to have to
be a life long learner.

00:27:07.140 --> 00:27:09.610
And so it's a great time to, kind of,

00:27:09.610 --> 00:27:13.220
take that agency on your own.

00:27:13.220 --> 00:27:16.060
So, that could be a silver lining as well.

00:27:16.060 --> 00:27:17.310
But, obviously if I were to list

00:27:17.310 --> 00:27:19.930
a lot of the suboptimal
things right now (chuckling)

00:27:19.930 --> 00:27:21.490
the list would get long.

00:27:21.490 --> 00:27:24.163
But, good question to focus
on the silver linings.

00:27:25.500 --> 00:27:26.640
So, let's see.

00:27:26.640 --> 00:27:30.147
There's a question from
Facebook Saif Siddiqui asks:

00:27:30.147 --> 00:27:31.967
"Can you please add subjects of commerce

00:27:31.967 --> 00:27:34.140
"as per Indian curriculum, please?"

00:27:34.140 --> 00:27:36.980
So Saif, that's actually a
subject close to my heart

00:27:36.980 --> 00:27:40.860
as I described my career
before Khan Academy

00:27:40.860 --> 00:27:42.322
was I was a financial analyst

00:27:42.322 --> 00:27:43.800
and so
(chuckling)

00:27:43.800 --> 00:27:45.140
we actually do have,

00:27:45.140 --> 00:27:47.400
it's not mapped to the Indian curriculum,

00:27:47.400 --> 00:27:50.230
but I will say if you want the intuition

00:27:50.230 --> 00:27:52.970
of finance and accounting
Khan Academy does have

00:27:52.970 --> 00:27:55.090
some resources for you already.

00:27:55.090 --> 00:27:56.930
So feel free to look at that content.

00:27:56.930 --> 00:27:58.010
I made a lot of that, actually,

00:27:58.010 --> 00:27:59.500
when I was a financial analyst

00:27:59.500 --> 00:28:01.060
and I saw that, you know,

00:28:01.060 --> 00:28:03.240
even some of the junior
analysts we were hiring

00:28:03.240 --> 00:28:06.180
who went to fancy colleges with 4.0 GPAs

00:28:06.180 --> 00:28:09.207
when I asked them very
basic questions like,

00:28:09.207 --> 00:28:11.027
"Okay, there's two identical houses.

00:28:11.027 --> 00:28:13.007
"The rent on one house is x,

00:28:13.007 --> 00:28:14.947
"the other house costs y to buy.

00:28:14.947 --> 00:28:16.640
"Which one should I buy, rent or buy?"

00:28:16.640 --> 00:28:18.660
And they'd say, "Oh I'd buy
y, I'd buy the second house".

00:28:18.660 --> 00:28:19.493
And they're like,

00:28:19.493 --> 00:28:21.287
"Okay now that price has
doubled on the second house

00:28:21.287 --> 00:28:23.107
"while the rent is the
same on the first house.

00:28:23.107 --> 00:28:23.940
"Now, which one?"

00:28:23.940 --> 00:28:25.570
And they're like, "Oh, you're right."

00:28:25.570 --> 00:28:28.070
And, so we try to give
those types of frameworks

00:28:28.070 --> 00:28:31.700
of how you can think, how
very seemingly complex

00:28:31.700 --> 00:28:33.500
situations in the economy or finance

00:28:33.500 --> 00:28:35.860
could be broken down into a fairly simple

00:28:35.860 --> 00:28:36.700
and intuitive framework.

00:28:36.700 --> 00:28:39.070
So, definitely check
some of that content out.

00:28:39.070 --> 00:28:41.610
And I hope in the future
we can do something

00:28:41.610 --> 00:28:44.460
like a more formal finance,
accounting, capital markets

00:28:44.460 --> 00:28:46.410
type course on Khan Academy.

00:28:46.410 --> 00:28:48.720
We actually have a lot of
personal finance content.

00:28:48.720 --> 00:28:50.580
It's a partnership with Bank of America

00:28:50.580 --> 00:28:52.150
around better money habits.

00:28:52.150 --> 00:28:54.180
We also have some career
content around that.

00:28:54.180 --> 00:28:55.993
So check that content out as well.

00:28:57.460 --> 00:28:58.893
So let's see.

00:28:59.870 --> 00:29:01.790
There is a question.

00:29:01.790 --> 00:29:04.693
YouTube ExplorerQuestioner.

00:29:04.693 --> 00:29:07.660
Oh it's a testimonial, well that's nice.

00:29:07.660 --> 00:29:09.990
You're saying, "You're amazing Sal".

00:29:09.990 --> 00:29:10.823
You're being generous.

00:29:10.823 --> 00:29:13.137
"Thank you for giving me the
opportunity to better myself

00:29:13.137 --> 00:29:16.250
"and make myself useful, and
find more purpose in my life."

00:29:16.250 --> 00:29:19.100
Well, ExplorerQuestioner on YouTube

00:29:19.100 --> 00:29:20.517
that's why all of us here at Khan Academy,

00:29:20.517 --> 00:29:22.830
and to be clear Khan Academy
is much more than me,

00:29:22.830 --> 00:29:24.180
much more than me and Dan, (chuckling)

00:29:24.180 --> 00:29:25.920
there's 200 plus other folks

00:29:27.160 --> 00:29:29.970
who are doing a lot of work
and thousands of people,

00:29:29.970 --> 00:29:31.990
but that's why we wanted
to dedicate our lives

00:29:31.990 --> 00:29:32.823
to this mission.

00:29:32.823 --> 00:29:37.290
Because, you know, at
the end if we can empower

00:29:37.290 --> 00:29:40.600
millions of folks that's
going to have a multiplier,

00:29:40.600 --> 00:29:41.470
or billions of folks,

00:29:41.470 --> 00:29:43.900
that's going to have a
multiplier effect for the world.

00:29:43.900 --> 00:29:46.520
You know, I sometimes
daydream that, you know,

00:29:46.520 --> 00:29:49.390
the person who one day
can find the vaccine

00:29:49.390 --> 00:29:52.880
for the next pandemic, or
solves an energy crisis,

00:29:52.880 --> 00:29:55.640
or brokers peace between nations,

00:29:55.640 --> 00:29:58.497
in a not too far off future says,

00:29:58.497 --> 00:29:59.887
"Yes and I'm in this position

00:29:59.887 --> 00:30:01.317
"because I was able to get my start,

00:30:01.317 --> 00:30:03.620
"or with some help from Khan Academy."

00:30:03.620 --> 00:30:05.420
So it really is motivating for us

00:30:05.420 --> 00:30:08.230
to hear those types of things.

00:30:08.230 --> 00:30:10.547
So from YouTube QualityMaterial asks:

00:30:10.547 --> 00:30:13.587
"Firstly, Mr. Sal, how are you
dealing with the quarantine?

00:30:13.587 --> 00:30:15.200
"Hope you're doing well."

00:30:15.200 --> 00:30:19.572
So, QualityMaterial, Mr.
Material maybe I should call you,

00:30:19.572 --> 00:30:21.170
(chuckling)

00:30:21.170 --> 00:30:23.140
thanks for asking, I'm doing fine.

00:30:23.140 --> 00:30:26.850
You know, obviously this is a
challenging time for everyone.

00:30:26.850 --> 00:30:30.830
But I remind myself the whole
world is going through this

00:30:30.830 --> 00:30:33.490
and relatively speaking I find myself

00:30:33.490 --> 00:30:35.970
in a very fortunate situation.

00:30:35.970 --> 00:30:40.793
You know, knock on wood I have
my health, my family does.

00:30:42.410 --> 00:30:45.810
We have a backyard, we have a park nearby

00:30:45.810 --> 00:30:50.160
that we can do to, staying
socially distanced appropriately.

00:30:50.160 --> 00:30:53.720
My kids are, I'm really
enjoying time with my kids.

00:30:53.720 --> 00:30:55.180
You know, it was hard at first,

00:30:55.180 --> 00:30:58.160
trying to do work and at they're
screaming in the next room.

00:30:58.160 --> 00:31:00.221
But we've kinda got a new normal now

00:31:00.221 --> 00:31:02.900
and once again, it reminded me, like,

00:31:02.900 --> 00:31:04.060
sometimes when you're doing work

00:31:04.060 --> 00:31:05.747
and your child is screaming, or saying,

00:31:05.747 --> 00:31:07.960
"Dada, dada, dada, let me show
you the thing that I made!"

00:31:07.960 --> 00:31:08.793
You're kinda like,

00:31:08.793 --> 00:31:10.687
"Oh, wait, wait, go away for a second

00:31:10.687 --> 00:31:12.930
"I need to finish this
really important E-mail."

00:31:12.930 --> 00:31:14.440
But there's something about this crisis

00:31:14.440 --> 00:31:15.273
that makes you say,

00:31:15.273 --> 00:31:17.277
"Well maybe this E-mail isn't as important

00:31:17.277 --> 00:31:19.967
"as hanging out with my eight year old"

00:31:19.967 --> 00:31:21.550
or, or whatever, so.
(chuckling)

00:31:21.550 --> 00:31:23.660
I've actually enjoyed it

00:31:23.660 --> 00:31:27.070
to kind of recenter
myself in a lot of ways.

00:31:27.070 --> 00:31:27.923
I think my wife and I, you know,

00:31:27.923 --> 00:31:29.840
were were just talking yesterday about

00:31:31.780 --> 00:31:34.360
having all of various events,

00:31:34.360 --> 00:31:36.810
or social things, or
things you have to go,

00:31:36.810 --> 00:31:38.920
travel, all get canceled

00:31:38.920 --> 00:31:41.610
and just us being home having quality time

00:31:42.720 --> 00:31:44.090
it's kind of nice.

00:31:44.090 --> 00:31:46.830
So I consider myself very fortunate.

00:31:46.830 --> 00:31:49.490
Obviously I have a job where,

00:31:49.490 --> 00:31:51.020
not only do I have a job now,

00:31:51.020 --> 00:31:52.970
a lot of people who are
working in restaurants,

00:31:52.970 --> 00:31:55.040
and other, you know, they're
losing their jobs now.

00:31:55.040 --> 00:31:57.820
So for those of us who
are able to keep working

00:31:57.820 --> 00:32:00.490
and keep having an income,
incredibly fortunate.

00:32:00.490 --> 00:32:02.390
And I consider myself fortunate

00:32:02.390 --> 00:32:03.810
that we have a role to play,

00:32:03.810 --> 00:32:07.080
to help in the situation
on the education dimension

00:32:07.080 --> 00:32:08.106
and getting information out dimension.

00:32:08.106 --> 00:32:13.106
So, thanks for asking, but I
consider myself very lucky.

00:32:13.540 --> 00:32:16.123
And I hope you are doing
well as well Mr. Material.

00:32:17.680 --> 00:32:21.160
So, we are almost out, we
actually are out of time.

00:32:21.160 --> 00:32:22.840
I'm maybe having too much fun,

00:32:22.840 --> 00:32:26.340
but we're here every day
and we're gonna do more

00:32:26.340 --> 00:32:29.400
of these just super
open-ended Ask Me Anythings.

00:32:29.400 --> 00:32:31.313
I'm really enjoying the questions.

00:32:32.180 --> 00:32:34.730
And all I'll say is thanks for joining.

00:32:34.730 --> 00:32:36.760
I hope everyone has a
really really good weekend,

00:32:36.760 --> 00:32:38.310
and I'll just remind folks

00:32:38.310 --> 00:32:40.010
if you're in a position to do so

00:32:40.010 --> 00:32:41.440
we are a not-for-profit,

00:32:41.440 --> 00:32:42.910
we are running at a deficit,

00:32:42.910 --> 00:32:45.120
and every donation matters.

00:32:45.120 --> 00:32:47.870
So anything you could do to
help support Khan Academy

00:32:47.870 --> 00:32:49.440
would mean a lot.

00:32:49.440 --> 00:32:51.443
Thank you and stay safe and healthy.

